# Loose ends

The last resort. A finding is fixed in the order that found it; one clearly out of that order's scope,
or crossing into a new scope, becomes a work-order or a parked work-order (`status: parked`, `trigger:`).
A row here is the rarest form: it passes all five tests (concrete; a known small fix; self-contained;
not owned; a stated out-of-scope reason it cannot be done now) and fits none of those. Nothing is dropped.

Only the orchestrator writes it (`loose_ends.py add --fix ... --why-not-now ...`). A row is at most 300
characters, its fix and reason included. Drained after an order resolves, at each checkpoint, and on
request ("drain the loose ends"). State is one of `new`, `proposed: <kind>` (the drain's; its file in
`drain/pending/` is an attachment), `waiting: <question>` or `declined: <reason>`. A routed or closed item is
removed with `drop --to WO-NNN | WO-NNN/SN | brain:<page> | closed:<reason>` and git keeps the
history: not on the list means routed.

